{
  "title": "Series Circuits",
  "order": 8,
  "pageType": "markdown",
  "content": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Series Circuits"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Introduction"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In a series circuit, all components are connected in a single loop—there's only one path for current to flow. Old-fashioned Christmas lights were often wired in series: if one bulb blew, the whole string went out! Understanding series circuits is essential for analysing more complex electrical systems."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Key Concept: ",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "In a series circuit: current is the same everywhere, potential differences add up to the supply voltage, and total resistance is the sum of all individual resistances."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Key Concepts"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What is a Series Circuit?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In a "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "series circuit",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", components are connected end-to-end in a single path."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "All current flows through every component"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Only one route from positive to negative terminal"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If one component breaks, the whole circuit stops"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "imageEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "src": "https://pub-5ac955cb5fd64af5baaae90d331db66d.r2.dev/images/2fdf8350-Series_circuit.svg",
          "alt": "Series circuit diagram showing three resistors connected end-to-end in a single loop with a battery",
          "width": "75%",
          "align": "center"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rules for Series Circuits"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Current"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Current is the SAME at all points in a series circuit.",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$I_1 = I_2 = I_3 = ... = I_{total}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Why? There's only one path for charge to flow. What goes in must come out—charge is conserved."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "An ammeter placed anywhere in the circuit reads the same value."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Potential Difference"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The total p.d. equals the SUM of p.d.s across each component.",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$V_{total} = V_1 + V_2 + V_3 + ...$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The supply voltage is shared between all components. Each component gets a 'share' of the voltage."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Resistance"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Total resistance is the SUM of individual resistances.",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$R_{total} = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + ...$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Adding resistors in series increases the total resistance because current must pass through all of them."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Quick Check"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quizEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "quizId": "check-series-circuits"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Analysing Series Circuits"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Step-by-Step Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Find total resistance: "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$R_T = R_1 + R_2 + ...$"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use Ohm's Law to find current: "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$I = V_{supply}/R_T$"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Current is the same throughout"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Find p.d. across each component: "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$V = IR$"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Check: p.d.s should add up to supply voltage"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Voltage Division"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The voltage divides in proportion to resistance:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$\\frac{V_1}{V_2} = \\frac{R_1}{R_2}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Larger resistance → larger share of voltage"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "For any component in series:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$V_1 = V_{total} \\times \\frac{R_1}{R_{total}}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Worked Examples"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Example 1:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Two resistors of 4 Ω and 8 Ω are connected in series to a 6 V battery. Calculate: a) The total resistance b) The current in the circuit c) The p.d. across each resistor"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Solution:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " a) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$R_{total} = 4 + 8 = 12$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Ω"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "b) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{6}{12} = 0.5$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "c) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$V_1 = IR_1 = 0.5 \\times 4 = 2$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V    "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$V_2 = IR_2 = 0.5 \\times 8 = 4$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Check: "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$2 + 4 = 6$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V ✓"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Example 2:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Three identical lamps are connected in series to a 12 V supply. If the current is 2 A, find: a) The resistance of each lamp b) The p.d. across each lamp"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Solution:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " a) Total resistance: "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$R_T = V/I = 12/2 = 6$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Ω    Each lamp: "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$R = 6/3 = 2$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Ω"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "b) Each lamp gets equal share (identical resistances):    "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$V = 12/3 = 4$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V each"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Example 3:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A 3 Ω resistor, 6 Ω resistor, and a lamp are connected in series to a 15 V supply. The current is 1 A. Find: a) The p.d. across each resistor b) The p.d. across the lamp c) The resistance of the lamp"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Solution:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " a) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$V_{3Ω} = IR = 1 \\times 3 = 3$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V    "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$V_{6Ω} = IR = 1 \\times 6 = 6$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "b) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$V_{lamp} = 15 - 3 - 6 = 6$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "c) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$R_{lamp} = V/I = 6/1 = 6$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Ω"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Example 4:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A 12 V battery is connected to a 100 Ω resistor and an LED in series. The LED requires 2 V to operate. Calculate: a) The p.d. across the resistor b) The current through the circuit"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Solution:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " a) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$V_{resistor} = 12 - 2 = 10$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " V"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "b) "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$I = V/R = 10/100 = 0.1$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A = 100 mA"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Advantages and Disadvantages"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Advantages"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Simple to wire"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Same current through all components"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Adding components increases total resistance (can limit current)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Disadvantages"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If one component fails, all stop working"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Components must share the voltage"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Higher resistance means less current for given voltage"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Exam Tip: ",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "In series circuits, the voltage is shared between components in proportion to their resistance. The component with the highest resistance gets the largest share of the voltage."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "comparisonTable",
        "attrs": {
          "title": "Series vs Parallel Circuits",
          "leftHeader": "Series Circuit",
          "rightHeader": "Parallel Circuit",
          "leftColor": "neutral",
          "rightColor": "neutral",
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Current",
              "left": "Same through all components",
              "right": "Splits between branches"
            },
            {
              "label": "Potential Difference",
              "left": "Shared between components (adds up to supply)",
              "right": "Same across each branch"
            },
            {
              "label": "Total Resistance",
              "left": "Sum of all resistances (R_total = R1 + R2 + ...)",
              "right": "Less than the smallest individual resistance"
            },
            {
              "label": "If One Component Fails",
              "left": "Entire circuit breaks — all components stop",
              "right": "Other branches continue to work"
            },
            {
              "label": "Adding Components",
              "left": "Increases total resistance, reduces current",
              "right": "Decreases total resistance, increases total current"
            },
            {
              "label": "Common Uses",
              "left": "Simple switches, fuses, fairy lights",
              "right": "Household wiring, car electrics"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Applications"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Voltage Dividers (Potential Dividers)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Two resistors in series can divide a voltage:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$V_{out} = V_{in} \\times \\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Used in:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Volume controls"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Sensor circuits with thermistors/LDRs"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reference voltage generation"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Current Limiting"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Resistors in series with LEDs limit current:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
